# Copy to .env.local. Everything here is read by scripts/serve.mjs, scripts/dev-session.mjs
# and npm run verify; nothing in this file is ever sent to a browser unless it says so.

# ── Required — the server refuses to boot without these two ───────────────────────────
DSX_DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost/shortdrama_dev
# The symmetric key the backend VERIFIES every bearer token against. It never issues one:
# scripts/dev-session.mjs signs the local viewer and operator with this same secret, and a
# real deployment points its identity provider at it (docs/auth.md). Change it and every
# existing token is refused, which is the intended behaviour.
DSX_JWT_SECRET=change-me-local-only

# ── Optional — server-to-server caller key for the internal routes ────────────────────
# The second way past a `reach=""` gateway, for a caller that has no account: a cron job,
# a seeder, another service. Hosted lanes set a real one.
DSX_INTERNAL_KEY=change-me-local-only

# Optional — where the built site lives (the default).
DSX_SITE_DIR=dist

# ── Stripe: the WEB storefront ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TEST-MODE keys from dashboard.stripe.com/test/apikeys. Leave both unset and the Store
# shows the server's honest refusal instead of a payment sheet; nothing else changes.
#   STRIPE_KEY          secret. Server only. Creates and confirms PaymentIntents.
#   STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE  publishable by design — it is handed to the payment sheet, and it
#                       is the ONE value in this file that is meant to reach a browser.
#STRIPE_KEY=sk_test_...
#STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE=pk_test_...

# ── RevenueCat: the NATIVE store lane ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Stripe is the web lane ONLY. App Store 3.1.1 names in-game currencies and premium-content
# unlocks as in-app-purchase products, so coins and the VIP pass go through the platform
# store inside an iOS or Android build, and RevenueCat is what lets a declared server action
# verify such a purchase (server/store.dsx `settleNative` says why it is the only provider
# one can reach). Both are unset on purpose: a placeholder here reads as a wired
# integration, and `storeCatalog.native.ready` is false without the first one, which is what
# makes the app REFUSE to open a purchase it could not honour rather than sell blind.
#
#   REVENUECAT_KEY              THE SECRET API KEY, and the one value in this file that
#                               must NEVER reach a client — not the app bundle, not a
#                               build-time constant, not a payload. It is a full-account
#                               credential: it reads and writes any subscriber, so a copy
#                               in a shipped binary is a copy anyone can extract. The
#                               backend uses it once, server-to-server, as
#                               `Authorization: Bearer` on a single GET to
#                               api.revenuecat.com/v1/subscribers/<subject> — that read is
#                               the whole trust boundary: RevenueCat has already validated
#                               the receipt with Apple or Google before it answers, so the
#                               app never parses a receipt and never takes the client's
#                               word for a purchase. The SDK key a mobile app embeds is a
#                               DIFFERENT, public key and does not belong in this file.
#
#   REVENUECAT_WEBHOOK_SECRET   The INBOUND half, and it is a shared value rather than a
#                               signing key: RevenueCat authenticates its webhook by
#                               echoing this string in the `Authorization` header, with no
#                               signature over the body. That is why the receiver declares
#                               `verify="bearer"` (server/store.dsx) — the framework names
#                               the weaker scheme for what it is instead of implying an
#                               HMAC. Two consequences: pick a long random value, because
#                               it is a password and not a digest, and set the SAME string
#                               in the RevenueCat dashboard's webhook Authorization field
#                               before the endpoint goes live — a receiver whose secret
#                               nobody echoes rejects every delivery and looks healthy
#                               doing it. Rotate by listing a second env name on the
#                               <webhook> row, so both are accepted during the overlap.
#
# Without either one the app still builds, still sells on the web, and refuses the native
# purchase in words (Components/Store.dsx). That refusal is the honest state, not a bug.
#REVENUECAT_KEY=sk_...
#REVENUECAT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=a-long-random-string-you-also-paste-into-revenuecat
